{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"CORRECTION TO THIS TICKET'S MECHANISM — the title and original description overstate what happens.\n\nI wrote that 'every inbound DC ghost-identity mint fails silently'. That is WRONG. The mint is never\nATTEMPTED on the DC path, so nothing fails there today.\n\nWHAT I MISSED: _ensure_inbound_assignee_identity (apply_inbound_records.py:50) guards on accountId:\n\n    account_id = assignee.get('accountId')\n    if not (isinstance(account_id, str) and account_id.strip()):\n        return\n\nand adapters/jira_family/identity_model.py:26-27 says plainly 'DC has no accountId at all'. DC user\nobjects carry name/key (REST v2), never accountId, and the function is called with the RAW Jira user\nobject (apply_inbound_records.py:155 and :321, both '_ensure_inbound_assignee_identity(\nfields[\"assignee\"], repo_root)'). So on a DC deployment the function returns at the guard and\nensure_identity_for is never reached.\n\nWHAT IS STILL TRUE, and it is two separate things:\n\n 1. THE ACTIVE GAP: DC inbound assignees never mint a ghost identity AT ALL — not because the mint\n    fails, but because the guard requires an identifier DC never supplies. Cloud gets ghost identities\n    on inbound; DC silently does not.\n\n 2. THE LATENT LANDMINE: the ValueError is real and I proved it at the API —\n      creation_channel='jira-datacenter' -> ValueError: invalid creation_channel 'jira-datacenter';\n      must be one of ['cli','import','jira','mcp','python']\n    It is simply unreachable while the accountId guard short-circuits first. It would fire the moment\n    anyone extends the guard to accept DC's name — i.e. the moment someone fixes (1) without also\n    fixing the channel. That is the trap worth documenting: the obvious one-line fix to (1) turns a\n    silent no-op into a swallowed exception, which is strictly worse to diagnose.\n\nSo a COMPLETE fix is both halves together: teach the guard DC's 'name' as the external id when\naccountId is absent, AND map the backend vendor to its family channel so the resulting mint validates.\nEither alone leaves DC without identities.\n\nThe acceptance criteria as written still hold — 'an inbound apply under the DC backend MINTS a ghost\nidentity successfully, asserted by a test that FAILS against today's code' is satisfied by neither\nhalf alone, which is the right bar. Only the stated mechanism was wrong.\n\nHOW I CAUGHT IT: I was about to write the RED test and checked whether the DC path actually reaches\nthe failing call, rather than assuming my API-level repro implied reachability. It did not. A repro\nthat proves a function rejects an input says nothing about whether that input ever arrives."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785470104223148001,"uuid":"82772aeb-7f78-4d7a-8bb9-37cc61443130"}